==Evolution==
Evolves into '''Hacker Cat''' at level 10.

Evolves into '''Cyberpunk Cat''' at level 30 using [[Catfruit]] and XP.

==Strategy/Usage==
==='''Normal Form'''===
Nerd Cat has Single Target attacks with mediocre damage, but has more effective range than the other two forms, making it able to [[cheese]] certain stages by using the enemy limit. However, in most other cases it's eclipsed by the unit's other forms and isn't worth using in general, especially considering his extremely long recharge time.

==='''Evolved Form'''===
Hacker Cat has the same qualities of Nerd Cat. Despite the reduced effective range, it can still occasionally chip enemies with its weak area attacks, especially against threats your units struggle to reach, such as [[Camelle]] in [[Chubby B. Goode]] and [[Manic Flying Cat (Enemy)|Manic Flying Cat]] in [[Forest Beasts (Deadly)|Forest Beasts]].

==='''True Form'''===
While the extra damage is always good, the guaranteed slow on hit transforms Cyberpunk Cat from a niche source of extra damage to among the best supportive units in the game. While he has a poor ~25% uptime, this is where Cyberstacking comes in. By stalling peons in his blindspot, you could accumulate multiple Cyberpunk Cats before triggering the boss wave on most basehit stages, allowing you to permanently slow the enemies and win easily. This strategy works as long as the main threat of the stage spawns on basehit, is affected by slow and there are stallable peons.

Cyberpunk forms a strong combination with [[Sniper the Recruit (Super Rare Cat)|Sniper the Deadeye]], who shares a similar range and can push enemy pushers out of Cyberpunk's blindspot and further control them.

Despite his strength, Cyberpunk is sluggish and is much worse on stages where the main threat spawns on a timer. He also retains his immense frailty and is vulnerable to certain enemies who may be able to hit him, such as [[Hackey]].

==='''Talents'''===
Cyberpunk's Slow talent is undoubtedly the most important, as it essentially doubles his ability duration when fully upgraded and greatly improves uptime. This improves Cyberstacking strategies, allowing only 2 desynced Cyberpunk Cats to have perfect slow uptime. It also heavily improves his performance as a supportive unit in his own right, especially on stages where the main threat spawns on a timer, since a single Cyberpunk Cat with the Slow talent maxed out has 50% slow uptime on his own, drastically reducing reliance on Cyberstacking to get value. If you use Cyberpunk Cat, this talent is crucial to get.

As for other talents, the situational [[Survive]] talent, when maxed, is useful too, since it allows Cyberpunk to reposition once. [[Barrier Breaker]] is unnecessary due to the presence of vastly superior alternatives that are far more versatile. Cost Down, however, isn't so relevant, since he remains affordable on most stages that do allow for his use even without it, though makes him easier to use on certain aggressive stages.

The last talent is a standard Attack Buff, which like all other stat boost talents is helpful, but in no way necessary due to his low base damage and high NP cost for what it does.

==='''Orb'''===
Because Cyberpunk is reliant on his crowd-control instead of his health or damage, all Effect Orbs are meaningless. A Shortened Cooldown Orb would work best on him, as even just a B rank Orb would be enough to shave off around 11 seconds from his lengthy cooldown.

